WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.733
The p-value.

2
00:00:02.033 --> 00:00:05.462
Everyone reports it, and most people get its meaning wrong.

3
00:00:05.462 --> 00:00:06.333
Let's fix that.

4
00:00:07.533 --> 00:00:13.700
The p-value is the probability of seeing data at least this extreme, if the null hypothesis were true.

5
00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:16.554
Small p means: hard to explain as luck.

6
00:00:16.554 --> 00:00:17.667
That's all it is.

7
00:00:18.867 --> 00:00:21.367
Here is every result pure chance could produce.

8
00:00:21.667 --> 00:00:23.678
Our result sits out on the right.

9
00:00:23.678 --> 00:00:27.943
The shaded tail is how often chance alone would do at least this well.

10
00:00:27.943 --> 00:00:29.467
That area is the p-value.

11
00:00:30.667 --> 00:00:32.067
Here's the common mistake.

12
00:00:32.367 --> 00:00:35.233
P is a statement about the data, assuming the null.

13
00:00:35.533 --> 00:00:38.133
It is not the probability that the null is true.

14
00:00:38.433 --> 00:00:41.967
And one minus p is certainly not the chance your change works.

15
00:00:43.167 --> 00:00:44.640
There's a bigger trap.

16
00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:50.933
At a threshold of zero point zero five, one in twenty truly null experiments succeeds by luck.

17
00:00:51.233 --> 00:00:55.733
Run a hundred tests with no real effect, and about five will look like wins.

18
00:00:56.933 --> 00:00:58.867
Here is that experiment in Python.

19
00:00:59.167 --> 00:01:04.333
Each round draws two samples from identical distributions, so there's no real effect.

20
00:01:04.633 --> 00:01:08.054
We count how often p dips below point zero five.

21
00:01:08.054 --> 00:01:09.408
Roughly five times.

22
00:01:09.408 --> 00:01:10.833
Luck alone succeeds.

23
00:01:12.033 --> 00:01:13.800
Here's how it bites in practice.

24
00:01:14.100 --> 00:01:19.200
Your RAGAS eval runs eight metrics across four categories, thirty two comparisons.

25
00:01:19.500 --> 00:01:25.533
At point zero five, about one point six of them look significant even for a change that does nothing.

26
00:01:25.833 --> 00:01:29.405
So one p of point zero four is likely the lottery.

27
00:01:29.405 --> 00:01:33.333
Pre-register one metric, or divide alpha by thirty two.

28
00:01:34.533 --> 00:01:35.276
Let's recap.

29
00:01:35.276 --> 00:01:39.300
The p-value is how surprising the data is, if the null were true.

30
00:01:39.600 --> 00:01:42.200
It is not the probability that the null is true.

31
00:01:42.500 --> 00:01:46.233
One in twenty null tests pass by luck at point zero five.

32
00:01:46.533 --> 00:01:49.679
So when you make many comparisons, correct for them.

33
00:01:49.679 --> 00:01:51.433
Next up: Confidence interval.
